// THE srv1 MICROVM SLOTS' CHARGE IS RELEASED BY A READING THE LANE NEVER WRITES (filed by
// still-otter-622, 2026-11-09, under the operator decision
// gunbc.design_ruling srv1_microvm_slot_charge_waits_for_realization). The standing
// gunbc.runner.runner_slot_allocation::MicrovmSlotRealization now decides whether an authored microVM
// slot's memory is charged against its host's runner envelope. srv1-13 and srv1-14 carry
// MicrovmSlotNotRealized rows read on 2026-11-09 (no controller, no slot instance, only the network
// ruleset active), which frees ~60-68 GiB and takes srv1 from a committed width of 4 to 8. Every arm of
// that standing is written BY HAND: the lane's install route
// (gunbc.live_deploy.apply microvm_controller_install_wet) reads back the tree receipt, the VMM receipt
// and whether systemd loads the slot template -- a positive read of realization, which is exactly what
// MicrovmSlotRealized means -- and then throws it away. So the charge returns only if someone edits a
// row, and the invalidation each row names is an event nothing performs on the row. That is the gap this

// CONVERGE RETIRES A WORKING SLOT BEFORE ITS REPLACEMENT HAS RUN A JOB. Surfaced by
// gunbc.design_ruling::srv3_srv4_fabric_slots_serve_ci (2026-11-09), which moved srv3-06/07 and
// srv4-06/07 from fabric execution to GitHub Actions: the live hosts then carry CI slots 01-05 and
// 08-20 (19 and 20 started by hand to deliver the promised 18) while the model commits 01-18. One pass
// of the converge entry therefore asks for 06 and 07 to be provisioned AND for 19 and 20 to be retired,
// in the same plan -- so the two runners that are serving today stop before the two that are meant to
// replace them have registered, let alone completed a job. Nothing in the ordering is wrong in the
// model; what is missing is the ORDER ITSELF: converge has no phase that reads back a completed job on
// a newly provisioned slot before it lets the retirement side of the same transition fire.
//
// This row is where that invariant is owned, not a caller's discipline. The operator is not running

// THE FCI-1 INSTRUMENT STILL BUILDS LABELS FROM AN UNBOUND SUBJECT. The 2026-11-09 review of the
// srv3/srv4 fabric-slot ruling found that gunbc.instruments.fabric_control_plane_live_probe::cp_probe_slot()
// was a TOTAL function over the refusal arms of its own subject gate, and ~29 call sites used it to build
// allocation keys, /opt/fabric-cells paths and systemctl unit names -- so "the caller checked the gate
// first" was the only thing between a refusal and an effect. That is what gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed
// forbids ("a failure arm refuses, never widens"; "making it unwritable" beats catching the wrong state).
// The effect surface is closed: the legs that mutate -- market reservation, the CAS attempt, the release
// commit, the host preconditions, the live cell observation, the systemctl read -- now take
// cp_probe_authorized_slot and act only on the identity BOUND by the roster's Selected arm, and a refusal
// projects a marker that names no host and no committed index, so it cannot name a cell.
// What remains is the label half: about twenty key/path/report builders still read the projection. Under a
// refusal they build marker-shaped labels, which is safe today only because the effects no longer consume
// them -- a fact about the callers, not about the types. Carrying the bound identity down those builders and
// deleting the total projection is the rung; it was not taken here because it changes the signature of

// A produced fn whose item body is a Branch or Match with a closure in tail position cannot reach
// the emitter, so the #16177/#16180 item-return move/Box::new boundary is unobservable for that
// form. Three independent upstream refusals block the shape; the emitter fixture becomes writable
// as soon as any one admits it. All sightings at origin/main b882c902; gunbc digests are per-dispatch

// PXE 2a (gunbc#16275, session proud-crab-189). The install's target disk is bound by observed serial in two
// places, and the change that added the second one is the change that records it
// (gunbc.design_argument::root_generalization: the answer belongs where the pattern was introduced).
data pxe_2a_base_revision: CommitSha = "ae3f13cead619854afadb36d449c44ba27b5d1fd"

// PXE 2b: the nbd install actuator's boot-once and the host-generic managed-host boot-once are two
// authorities that set a Redfish boot-source override and reset a host's BMC. The install actuator has NO
// managed-host identity (os_install_actuator_steps is keyed on arm+boot_control+export; network_boot_actuation
// carries boot_control+boot_target+arm shape and no host), so its boot-once controller is HARD-CODED to srv3
// in the realization -- a defect, not merely a style choice: a second managed host could not be booted once
// from its own row. The two answer different concerns (an OS-install sequence step vs a managed-fleet one-shot
// boot) but share the override+reset question, so until the identity is threaded they are recorded as a fork.
data nbd_boot_once_sighting_revision: CommitSha = "7aab0ecfc9b25efdeb5270b436e61a197e48791b"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE READING PATH MOVED TO `gunbc.integration_concern.reading`, which imports the derived roster:
// the entry point cannot sit here, because the roster imports this module for the row type. What
// stays here is every helper that is generic over rows -- what it renders, not which rows it is
// handed.

fn env_list(name: String) -> List<String> {
  match shell.Env.Get(name: name as NonEmptyStr).value {
    Absent => []
    Present { value: v } => filter(split(s: v, delimiter: ","), x => x != "")
  }
}

fn declaration_text(r: DeclarationRef) -> String {
  let base = concat(r.module_path as String, concat(" ", r.decl_name as String))
  match r.field {
    WholeDeclaration => base
    NamedField { field_name: f } => concat(base, concat(".", f as String))
    TypeParameter { name: n } => concat(base, concat("<", concat(n as String, ">")))
  }
}

fn evidence_text(e: SightingEvidence) -> String {
  match e {
    CitesDeclaration { declaration: d } => concat("declaration ", declaration_text(r: d))
    CitesHistorical { declaration: h } => join(["declaration ", h.module_path as String, " ", h.decl_name as String, " at ", h.at as String], "")
    CitesArtifact { label: l, sha256: d } => join(["artifact sha256 ", d.hex as String, " (", l as String, ")"], "")
  }
}

fn sighting_lines(g: ConcernSighting) -> List<String> {
  concat(
    [concat("  sighting at ", g.revision as String), concat("    account: ", g.account as String)],
    map(concat([g.evidence.head], g.evidence.tail), e => concat("    cites: ", evidence_text(e: e))),
  )
}

fn requirement_lines(k: ConcernKind) -> List<String> {
  match k {
    SuspectedFork { authorities: _ } => []
    SuspectedCapabilityGap { model: m, requirement: r, standing: st } => [
      concat("  model: ", declaration_text(r: m)),
      concat("  requirement: ", r as String),
      match st {
        RequirementEstablished { by: e } => concat("  requirement established by: ", evidence_text(e: e))
        RequirementUnestablished { decided_by: d } => concat("  requirement NOT established; decided by: ", d as String)
      },
    ]
  }
}

fn obligation_lines(d: ConcernDisposition) -> List<String> {
  match d {
    Triaged { home: _, preserve: ps, next: _ } => map(ps, o => join(["  preserve (", declaration_text(r: o.holder), "): ", o.statement as String], ""))
    _ => []
  }
}

fn concern_text(c: IntegrationConcern) -> String {
  join(concat(concat(concat(
    [
      concat("- ", c.identity as String),
      concat("  kind: ", kind_label(k: c.kind)),
      concat("  disposition: ", disposition_label(d: c.disposition)),
      concat("  question: ", c.question as String),
      concat("  modules: ", join(concern_modules(c: c), ", ")),
    ],
    requirement_lines(k: c.kind)),
    obligation_lines(d: c.disposition)),
    flat_map(concat([c.sightings.head], c.sightings.tail), g => sighting_lines(g: g))), "\n")
}

fn reading_text(title: String, rows: List<IntegrationConcern>) -> String {
  join(concat(concat([title], map(rows, c => concern_text(c: c))), concat(["unconnected routes:"], map(unconnected_routes, r => concat("- ", r)))), "\n")
}

